{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head 7f45a0aa240b3d1c4f9499e01f0a6817ac21de3b on\nrequest/submit-deadline-override, against the reporting artifact\ndocs/reference/gs/state.md (git:sha1...3e9d8e) and the whole 29-artifact set.\n\nThe substance of both security repairs is right, and five of the six coverage\nitems are genuinely closed — I re-proved each by my own mutation rather than by\nreading. But the head does not pass its own gate on my machine, one of my\nfindings is not actually closed, and the fix for another regressed the adapter's\nstartup order. Details below, with the commands and the exact output.\n\nGATES I RAN, at this exact head in the branch worktree, exit codes written by\nthe process into a file rather than read through a pipeline:\n\n  go vet ./...                            exit 0\n  go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...     exit 1\n\nG1. BLOCKING. The suite is red. cmd/gs failed after 555.892s:\n\n  --- FAIL: TestBatchReportsTheReplayKeyWhenAnActsDeadlineExpires (0.61s)\n      deadline_test.go:265: batch refusal does not say \"position 1\": batch\n      submit: Post \"http://127.0.0.1:53684/v0/submit\": context deadline exceeded\n      (Client.Timeout exceeded while awaiting headers). The act at position 0\n      may already have landed: the resident had 400ms to answer and did not. ...\n\nI did not leave that as one observation. The test passes 10 times out of 10 on a\nquiet machine (go test -count=10 -run ... ./cmd/gs/, exit 0, 13.456s). With 18\nbusy-loops running on an 18-core machine it fails 5 times out of 5 (exit 1), with\nthat same message every time. So it is not unreproducible: it is exactly\nreproducible, in both directions, on demand.\n\nThe cause is in the test's shape. It gives the in-process resident 400ms to\nsign, fold and compare-and-swap act 0 — an act that has to SUCCEED for the test\nto reach its subject — and 400ms is the only knob it has, because the deadline\nis per invocation and both acts share it. On a loaded machine the first act\ntimes out too, and the assertion about position 1 never gets its chance. A lane\nwhose whole premise is that a resident can be slower than a fixed short deadline\nshould not ship a test that assumes one is fast. Give the batch a generous\ndeadline and let the deliberate stall be the only thing that spends it; the\nstall does not need a short deadline to be observed, only patience.\n\nI would not accept \"it passes here\" for this one, because I can make it fail on\ndemand. Contrast the internal/app failure you recorded, which I do accept: I\nchecked it independently rather than take your word, and go list -deps\n./internal/app/ and go list -deps -test ./internal/app/ both contain no\nresidentclient, while git diff --name-only main..7f45a0aa touches no file under\ninternal/app. The lane cannot have caused it. Recording it by name instead of\ncalling it flaky on one observation was the right thing to do, and I would ask\nthe same treatment for G1 — except that G1 is yours to fix, not to record.\n\nG2. BLOCKING. C1 is not closed. TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline proves\nthat each of the twelve commands RESOLVES the deadline — the refusal is the\nresolver's — not that it KEEPS the context the resolver handed back. I reapplied\nthe exact mutation from my last review at this head: gs promise changed to\n\n  if _, err := withSubmitDeadline(ctx, set); err != nil { return err }\n\nwhich resolves, refuses a bad value, and throws the good one away. Every command\nstill refuses \"soon\", so the new test stays green, and so does the whole package:\ngo test ./cmd/gs/ exit 0, 543.401s. The mutation the repair was written to kill\nis still alive, and the test's name now asserts something it does not check.\n\nTwo shapes would close it. Drive each command against a stalled server under a\nshort --deadline and assert it gives up quickly — honest but twelve times the\ncost. Or gate it in source, the way internal/docset/surface.go already gates the\nflag table: an AST check that every function calling actFlags also binds the\ncontext withSubmitDeadline returns. The second is cheap and exact, and it is the\nsame trick that makes the \"twelve\" claim trustworthy today.\n\nG3. BLOCKING. The adapter's startup refusal now lands after it has joined the\nworkroom. At fe2d12d6, main() refused immediately after newServer. Now the\nrefusal is held on startupRefusal and answered by run() — but main() calls\nserver.attend(...) and starts server.heartbeat(...) first. I built the adapter at\nthis head and ran it with an unreadable value:\n\n  $ GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=eventually gitseq-mcp --actor codex --repo \u003ctmp\u003e\n  gitseq-mcp: no gitseq workroom for /var/.../tmp.Ck0L7eSJwm: ...\n  gitseq-mcp: GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE \"eventually\" is not a duration: ...\n\nThe attach is attempted first. Against a real workroom that attach announces\npresence and takes a lease, and the heartbeat goroutine runs until the deferred\nstop. So run()'s own comment (\"stops the adapter before it serves anything\"),\nthe report's C3 answer (\"before the adapter serves anything\"), and\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md (\"stops it before it serves anything, rather\nthan at the first call\") are each true of MCP frames and false of resident\ntraffic. One line in main() restores the order without giving up the tested\nguard in run().\n\nG4. BLOCKING, and this one is partly my fault. Moving the resolution into\nnewServer was my suggestion, and I did not weigh what it costs: newServer has 55\ncall sites in the adapter's tests, and every one of them now reads the real\nprocess environment. Two packages' suites depend on the ambient\nGITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE — the variable this very change tells operators to set.\n\n  GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=90s go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/    exit 1\n    --- FAIL: TestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation\n\n90s is a good value. It fails because main_test.go:2368 asserts\nnewServer(\"human\", \"\").deadlines == defaultResidentDeadlines, which the\nconstructor is now designed to vary. The guard and the feature contradict each\nother by construction, and an operator who follows the new documentation cannot\nrun this repository's tests.\n\n  GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=eventually go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/    exit 1\n    five failures, none about deadlines: TestEveryToolDeclaresAnOutputSchema-\n    ItsOwnResponsesSatisfy, TestStatelessDiscoverAndToolList, TestCommandLine-\n    AndAdapterResolveTheSameReferences, TestToolErrorResultStillCarriesLive-\n    Attention, TestStateToolDisclosesItsBases\n\n  GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=eventually go test ./cmd/gitseq-github/    exit 1\n    four failures: TestDryRunProposalDoesNotRequireLocalConnectorCustody,\n    TestTheCommandResolvesTheRouteBeforeReadingTheKey, TestTheCommandRoutes-\n    ObservationsToTheAdvertisedResident, TestAProposalDoesNotResolveThe-\n    ObservationRoute\n\ninternal/residentclient is clean under the same variable, because its tests set\nit with t.Setenv. Keep the environment read out of the constructor the tests\nuse: newServerFromEnvironment() for main, newServer(policy) for everyone else —\nor neutralise the variable in a TestMain. The connector has the same shape\nthrough run().\n\nWORDING, which you asked me to check. Branch 3 reads wrong.\n\n  Branch 1, every act keyed: \"Run the same file again — every act up to this one\n  carries an idempotency key, so what landed replays and nothing is appended\n  twice — or raise --deadline\". At 3am that is exactly what I would want:\n  it says what to do, why it is safe, and what the other option is.\n\n  Branch 2, failing act keyed but an earlier one not: \"Do not rerun the file yet:\n  the acts at position 1 carry no idempotency_key and would land a second time.\n  Look for this one under key \"publish-report\", give the others keys, or raise\n  --deadline\". Right, and rightly emphatic. One nit: \"the acts at position 1\" is\n  plural over a single position.\n\n  Branch 3, the failing act itself keyless: \"...would append a second copy of it,\n  and of every other keyless act at position 0: find them first...\". The list\n  includes the failing act's own position, so where it is the only keyless act —\n  the single-act batch, the ordinary case — it names itself as an \"other\". G1's\n  real output above is this branch in the wild: position 0 failed, and the\n  message points at position 0 as the other act to find. Either exclude position\n  from that list or drop the word \"other\".\n  TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix currently enshrines the defect.\n\nSMALLER THINGS, not blocking.\n\n  acts[position] is indexed with no bounds check, two lines below a loop that\n  bothers to guard index \u003c len(acts). Either position is trusted or it is not; a\n  panic in the recovery path is the worst possible moment for one.\n\n  The reporting artifact contradicts itself. Its first section answers my\n  findings correctly, but \"Conditions met\" below it is the old text: section 2\n  still says \"the startup wiring moved out of main() into\n  configureResidentDeadlines\", which is deleted at this head, and sections 3 and\n  4 still tell the pre-S1 batch story (\"the key to replay under is named\"; \"An\n  act with no idempotency_key cannot be replayed at all ... so that refusal says\n  to find the act first instead\"). The artifact is the durable account of this\n  change; a later reader gets two answers to the same question.\n\n  cmd/gitseq-github/deadline.go duplicates cmd/gs/deadline.go's context-key\n  carrier in a second package. Two copies is tolerable. A third would argue for\n  putting the carrier beside ResolveSubmitDeadline in residentclient.\n\nWHAT IS CLOSED, each re-proved by my own mutation rather than by reading:\n\n  S1. The rule is right: replay safety is a property of the prefix, and weighing\n  every act at or before the failure is the correct test. batch.md no longer\n  makes the unconditional claim. Only the branch-3 wording above stands.\n\n  S2. Closed. Removing the saturation guard turns TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAn-\n  AbsurdDeadline red (exit 1). I also checked that the saturated value is safe\n  downstream rather than assuming it: time.Time.Add strips the monotonic reading\n  instead of wrapping, and a context deadline in 2318 is an ordinary far-future\n  timer. On your question — no, I do not think the deadline needs an upper bound.\n  Saturation makes the backstop honest, the value is operator-set at the same\n  authority as the flag, and a ceiling would be a policy invented inside a\n  repair. Leave it out.\n\n  C2. Closed. Dropping \"\u0026\u0026 timeout.Timeout()\" now turns\n  TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal red (exit 1). At the\n  previous head the same mutation was green. The *net.OpError wrapping\n  ECONNREFUSED is the right witness.\n\n  C3's guard. Closed. Deleting the startupRefusal check in run() turns\n  TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment red (exit 1). The ordering\n  problem is G3, not this.\n\n  C4. Closed. Making the connector swallow the refusal turns\n  TestConnectorRefusesAnUnreadableDeadlineBeforeObserving red (exit 1).\n\n  C5 and C6 are as described.\n\n  Artifact set. All 29 stand at 7f45a0aa, each at its own path, each effective\n  and none stale — I inspected all 29 — and the set is exactly\n  git diff --name-only main..7f45a0aa, 29 paths, nothing missing and nothing\n  extra.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved, unchanged from my last conclusion, and re-checked\nagainst the delta rather than carried over. Affected layers are 3/4\n(internal/residentclient, the client-side resident boundary) and 7 (cmd/gs,\ncmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gitseq-github). The delta touches internal/residentclient\nonly in its test file. Nothing in docs/reference/architecture.md pins a\nsubmission deadline, a transport backstop, or where an adapter reads its\nconfiguration; the page's rows for the three surfaces describe what they\ncompose, not what they time out after. Nothing changes what the kernel admits,\nwhat the fold decides, what any projection says, or any dependency direction.\narchitecture.md is correctly absent from the set.\n\nSECURITY: the two defects I raised are substantively fixed, and I reproduced\nboth repairs rather than accept them. What now stands at this boundary is G3 —\nan adapter told to stop by its own configuration announces presence and takes a\nlease before it stops — and G4, which is a gate-integrity problem rather than a\nruntime one: the project's own test gate can be turned red, or green, by an\nenvironment variable the feature documents. Neither changes what is signed,\nadmitted or attributed, and nothing new enters a payload.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the shape remains right and I would not redesign it. The cut I\nwould make is the one behind G2 — replace the twelve-command refusal loop with a\nsource-level gate beside the existing docset surface gate, which is both smaller\nand stronger than what is there now. The duplicate carrier package noted above is\nthe only other place I would watch.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS APPROVABLE: G1, G2, G3 and G4, plus the branch-3 wording.\nThe two small items and the report's stale section I would take in the same\nround but would not hold the head for on their own. Nothing here is a\ndisagreement about design; every one of them is a claim that is not yet true of\nthe code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3bf76157d07112797d4c2edbf2bea215bc3e9d8e","binding":"assigned","head":"7f45a0aa240b3d1c4f9499e01f0a6817ac21de3b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0013173f26a7a0c2407ac312e1711c8322bdb061\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:46e1dcd4888166e1c7660cad46cfa0622a7ab3ab\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0013173f26a7a0c2407ac312e1711c8322bdb061","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}